<p>When I was younger, I thought I knew a lot of things.  I had a fantastic memory, and even if I didn't know the details about something, I would often know where to look.</p>
<p>I don't think I was ever afraid to admit that there were things I <b>didn't</b> know, though.  If I didn't know something, I would happily say "I don't know" or even "I can look into that for you".  In every case, I assumed that if someone asked me something, they asked <i>in the expectation</i> that I knew something about it.</p>
<p>(For you youngsters out there, I want to be clear that this is so long ago, there wasn't just no Google, there was no web; sure, the internet existed, but there was no useful means of extracting information using it.  If you wanted to know something, you had to either ask someone or find and consult a printed work. It was a different world.)</p>
<p>My wife is a teacher.  When I first met her, I was staggered by the alternative formulation she had for this: "I don't know, how about we find out together?"  Apparently, this is a common thing when you are dealing with children, but it was totally foreign to me.  I mean, either you know the answer or you don't.  What's the point of us both spending time looking into it?  That's half as efficient as one person finding out and telling the other person.  (Again, this is long before I'd heard of pair programming.)</p>
<p>As she explained it, I realized it made perfect sense in her context: after all, the key thing in teaching is not the <i>teaching</i> per se, but the <i>learning</i> that happens in the other person.  You don't just learn facts, you learn the <i>process</i> of uncovering facts.  And there is nothing like learning-by-doing.  Except of course, <i>guided</i> learning-by-doing, where you learn with the safety net of someone who's been there before and done it before.</p>
<p>Since then, I've read a fair bit of Plato, and that leads to an understanding of Socratic Dialogue.  The purpose of Socratic Dialogue is not, of course, to have a conversation between equals, but to have Socrates mock you while so utterly convincing you of his point of view that you believe that you have come up with it yourself.  I'm going to say, "it's the same thing".  Socrates <i>thinks</i> he knows the shape of the answer, but says he knows nothing, and then picks apart any pat answers that anyone else offers: that leaves room for a first-principles argument about the topic at hand as they "find out together".</p>
<p>I think it's fair to say that I have used both these techniques again and again in my career, helping people around me to not just "find out" what we do but to truly understand <i>from first principles</i> what the practices are, what the values are underlying them, and <i>why</i> these practices meet the principles better than anything else we have thought of <i>yet</i>.  (I always maintain an open mind that somebody could come up with something better; it's even happened from time to time.)</p>
<p>I claim that this technique - however you choose to apply it - is superior to either just giving the answer or simply saying "I don't know".  Why?</p>
<ul>
<li><p>It gives the person asking the question the opportunity to "solve it themselves", even if in fact you are just drip feeding them the answer by the probing questions you ask.</p>
<li><p>It fosters teamwork and collaboration.  These are things which most people are not good at (me included) and every opportunity we have to practice these <i>skills</i> is time well spent, even if if would be "more efficient" to just give the answer.</p>
<li><p>It is undeniable that something that you remember something better if you have had to figure it out rather than just beeing spoonfed it.  I don't know why this is, but I'm happy to figure it out together :-)</p>
<li><p>Saying you will work with someone on an issue, even if you genuinely don't know, shows compassion, caring and an interest in their life and problems.  This builds trust and relationships, and generally builds a more harmonious working environment.  On a more transactional level, it also gets you credit in "the relationship bank" for when you next want a favour from them.</p>
<li><p>It also encourages people to think more collaboratively.  It may have taken that person a lot of courage to ask for help, and if you just dismiss them with "I don't know", then they may not have what it takes to ask again; on the other hand, if you work on an issue with them, they are more likely to ask for help in the future.  This is particularly important if you are in a "one-up" relationship with them (i.e. they look up to you and think of you as their "superior").</p>
<li><p>By not just spouting a solution, it accepts that there may be a solution to this problem that you haven't thought of; and the dialogue leading to that solution may take you in a different direction from the one you originally envisaged.  In particular, they may be aspects to the problem which come out in the dialogue that did not occur to you initially that might either invalidate your solution or present one that is simpler.</p>
<li><p>While the answer to the question might not seem of immediate relevance to you, the fact that you go on this journey means that you will discover the answer.  If nothing else, you will know <b>something</b> new, but more importantly, you are practising <i>learning</i>, which is a skill we all need to keep at.</p>
<li><p>It's also superior to saying "I'll figure it out and get back to you" on the simple grounds that it's more fun doing things with other people than doing them by yourself.</p>
</ul>
<p>None of this is to say that it's bad to admit that you don't know something.  In general, it's best to live life in the certain knowledge that you don't really know anything (I refer you once again to Socrates).  Admitting this is fine.  What I no longer believe to be fine is brushing off a question with "I don't know".  That's actually not humility, it's arrogance: "I don't know and there's no reason I need to, so why should I help you find out?"  Not that I'm saying you consciously think that.  Just that, if you think about it, that <i>is</i> what you're saying.</p>
<p>Is there anything else I can help you with?</p>
